## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-key streaming stats with independent sliding windows, combining deques, hashtables, and a heap with lazy expirations. It mirrors real services that analyze per-entity signals under time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key deques for time-bounded data
- Hash map for key -> stats
- Max-heap with lazy deletion to keep top averages up-to-date
- Expiration handling driven by currentTime

## Code Example
```javascript
class PerKeyRollingStats {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.map = new Map(); this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  add(key, value, ts) {
    let rec = this.map.get(key) || { dq: [], sum: 0 };
    rec.dq.push([ts, value]);
    rec.sum += value;
    while (rec.dq.length && rec.dq[0][0] < ts - this.W) {
      const [t, v] = rec.dq.shift();
      rec.sum -= v;
    }
    this.map.set(key, rec);
    const avg = rec.sum / rec.dq.length;
    this.heap.push({ key, avg, validAt: ts, len: rec.dq.length });
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const rec = this.map.get(top.key);
      const avg = rec && rec.dq.length ? rec.sum / rec.dq.length : -Infinity;
      if (top.avg !== avg) continue; // stale entry
      res.push(top.key);
    }
    // optional: reinsert from a temp to preserve heap state
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a very large number of keys with memory constraints?
- How would you adapt to variable window sizes per key or out-of-order events?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a standard data structure with array-based tree, index math, and clear complexity expectations.

## Key Concepts
- Segment tree
- Range maximum
- Point updates
- Space/time complexity

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(arr) {
    this.n = arr.length;
    this.size = 1;
    while (this.size < this.n) this.size <<= 1;
    this.tree = new Array(this.size * 2).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i < this.n; i++) this.tree[this.size + i] = arr[i];
    for (let i = this.size - 1; i > 0; i--) this.tree[i] = Math.max(this.tree[i<<1], this.tree[i<<1|1]);
  }
  update(idx, val) {
    let pos = this.size + idx;
    this.tree[pos] = val;
    for (pos >>= 1; pos > 0; pos >>= 1) {
      this.tree[pos] = Math.max(this.tree[pos<<1], this.tree[pos<<1|1]);
    }
  }
  query(l, r) {
    let res = -Infinity;
    l += this.size; r += this.size;
    while (l <= r) {
      if (l & 1) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[l++]);
      if (!(r & 1)) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[r--]);
      l >>= 1; r >>= 1;
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

