- How would you adapt for non-strictly increasing sequences or allow leading zeros in some tokens?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of graph algorithms, state expansion techniques, and path reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm on expanded state space (node, used)
- Edge relaxation with and without discount application
- Path reconstruction across state transitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithDiscounts(n, edges, s, t, K) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>[]);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(null));

  const pq = new MinHeap();
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.insert([s,0,0]); // [node, used, distance]

  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [u,used,d] = pq.extractMin();
    if(d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if(u === t) break;

    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      // Without discount
      if(d + w < dist[v][used]){
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used,d+w]);
      }
      // With discount if available
      if(used < K && d < dist[v][used+1]){
        dist[v][used+1] = d;
        prev[v][used+1] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used+1,d]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Find optimal state at target
  let minDist = INF, minUsed = -1;
  for(let k=0;k<=K;k++){
    if(dist[t][k] < minDist){
      minDist = dist[t][k];
      minUsed = k;
    }
  }

  // Path reconstruction
  const path = [];
  let curr = [t,minUsed];
  while(curr && prev[curr[0]][curr[1]]){
    path.unshift(curr[0]);
    curr = prev[curr[0]][curr[1]];
  }
  if(curr[0] === s) path.unshift(s);

  return {distance: minDist, path};
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Unreachable target: return INF distance
- K=0: reduces to standard Dijkstra
- K larger than path length: effectively unlimited discounts
- Multiple optimal states: choose minimal used count

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model DP with obstacles and variable step sizes, requiring careful base cases and transition logic. It also assesses both memoization and tabulation familiarity.

## Key Concepts

- DP with multiple transitions (1..k)
- Handling blocked steps
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Time complexity: O(n*maxJump); Space: O(n) (or O(maxJump) with optimization)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Bottom-up tabulation
function countWays(n, maxJump, blocked) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) { dp[i] = 0; continue; }
    for (let j = 1; j <= maxJump; j++) {
      if (i - j >= 0) dp[i] += dp[i - j];
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

```javascript
// Memoized recursive version
function countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i = n, memo = {}) {
  if (i === 0) return 1;
  if (i < 0 || blocked[i]) return 0;
  if (memo[i] !== undefined) return memo[i];
  let ways = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= maxJump; t++) ways += countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i - t, memo);
  memo[i] = ways;
  return ways;
}
```

